    cdcLink: 'CDC BMI Guide',
    helpCenter: 'Help Center',
    faqTitle: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
    faqSubtitle: 'Everything you need to know about BMI calculations, metabolic ratios, accuracy, and athletic measurements.',
    sections: [
      { title: 'Understanding Your Body Mass Index', text: 'Body Mass Index (BMI) is a valuable, internationally recognized screening standard used by healthcare professionals worldwide. It groups your weight category relative to your height, giving you a quick reference point for metabolic and cardiovascular disease risks. Real BMI Calculator pairs this score with the standard classifications from the World Health Organization (WHO) and Centers for Disease Control (CDC) to offer you direct, clinical context.' },
      { title: 'How BMI, BMR & TDEE Connect Together', text: 'Your BMI indicates if your weight falls within a balanced window. Your BMR reveals the baseline metabolic energy your organs require at absolute rest. Finally, your TDEE integrates daily physical activities, showing you the exact calories required to maintain your current weight. Combining these three pillars gives you a comprehensive, customized map of your energy balance and weight-management path.' },
      { title: 'BMI & Cardiovascular Health: Heart Risk, Blood Pressure & Stroke Prevention', text: 'Cardiovascular research from global health authorities (including WHO, AHA, and National Heart Foundations) emphasizes that while BMI is a key baseline indicator for heart disease, stroke, and hypertension, it should always be evaluated alongside waist circumference, blood pressure, and cholesterol levels. Real BMI Calculator bridges this gap by offering integrated Waist-to-Hip Ratio, Heart Rate Zones, BMR, and TDEE tools to provide a complete cardiovascular and metabolic risk profile.' },
      { title: 'Type 2 Diabetes & Abdominal Adiposity Screening (Diabetes Canada Aligned)', text: 'According to Diabetes Canada and WHO clinical guidelines, elevated BMI (≥25.0 kg/m² or ≥23.0 kg/m² for Asian populations) and abdominal visceral adiposity directly correlate with increased risk of Type 2 Diabetes and Insulin Resistance. Monitoring your BMI alongside waist circumference offers early detection and vital metabolic risk prevention.' }
    ],
    whoTable: [
      { category: 'Underweight', range: '< 18.5', risk: 'Low (Increased Nutri. Risk)', status: 'underweight' },
      { category: 'Normal Weight', range: '18.5 – 24.9', risk: 'Minimal (Optimal Health)', status: 'normal' },
      { category: 'Overweight', range: '25.0 – 29.9', risk: 'Increased (Caution Recommended)', status: 'overweight' },
      { category: 'Obesity Class I', range: '30.0 – 34.9', risk: 'High (Moderate Risk)', status: 'obesity-1' },
      { category: 'Obesity Class II', range: '35.0 – 39.9', risk: 'Very High (Severe Risk)', status: 'obesity-2' },
      { category: 'Obesity Class III', range: '≥ 40.0', risk: 'Extremely High (Acute Risk)', status: 'obesity-3' }
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is the difference between standard BMI and Real BMI?', a: 'Standard BMI calculates body mass strictly using height and weight. Real BMI integrates standard BMI with WHO & CDC reference tables, gender-adjusted body fat estimates, BMR, and TDEE to provide a complete, clinical-grade overview of your body composition.' },
      { q: 'How is BMI calculated?', a: 'BMI compares your weight and height using a simple formula: weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared (kg/m²), or (weight in lbs / height in inches²) x 703 for imperial units.' },
      { q: 'How does BMI relate to heart disease and stroke risk?', a: 'Elevated BMI levels (25.0 kg/m² and above) correlate with higher incidence of hypertension, high LDL cholesterol, and type-2 diabetes — all major contributing risk factors for coronary heart disease and stroke.' },
      { q: 'Why is waist circumference important alongside BMI for heart health?', a: 'Excess abdominal visceral fat surrounding vital organs poses significantly greater cardiovascular risk than subcutaneous fat stored on limbs. Combining BMI with Waist-to-Hip ratio provides a far more accurate heart risk assessment.' },
      { q: 'What is a healthy BMI for men and women?', a: 'For most adults, a healthy BMI range is 18.5 to 24.9. However, WHO recommends a lower threshold of 18.5 to 22.9 for Asian and South Asian populations due to metabolic risk profiles.' },
      { q: 'Is BMI accurate for muscular people or athletes?', a: 'BMI is a screening tool, not a diagnostic measurement of body fat. Because dense muscle tissue weighs more than fat volume, muscular athletes may register a higher BMI score while maintaining very low body fat.' },
      { q: 'Why does WHO recommend lower BMI cutoffs for Asian populations?', a: 'Clinical research shows that South Asian and East Asian individuals tend to accumulate a higher percentage of abdominal visceral fat at lower body weights. Therefore, WHO defines overweight for Asian adults starting at 23.0 kg/m².' },
      { q: 'What does a BMI of 25 mean?', a: 'A BMI of 25 sits at the exact threshold between the healthy weight range and the overweight category. It serves as a practical reminder to assess waist circumference and physical activity habits.' },
      { q: 'What is BMR and why does it matter?', a: 'BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) is the energy your body expends at rest to support vital organ functions. Knowing your BMR helps establish baseline calorie requirements for weight management.' },
      { q: 'What is TDEE and why does it matter?', a: 'TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) estimates the total calories burned daily including movement and exercise, serving as the blueprint for maintaining, losing, or gaining weight.' }
    ]
